# sys_getrandom.tkr — the §16 fourth-crumb fixture. `main.tks` bump-allocates an 8-character
# placeholder buffer via `teko::mem::as_cstr`, bridges its opaque address to an `i64` word via
# `teko::sys::ptr_word`, and issues `teko::sys::syscall3(sys::SYS_GETRANDOM, <word>, 8, 0)` — the raw
# x86_64 Linux `getrandom(buf, 8, 0)` syscall — filling the first 8 bytes of the buffer with kernel
# entropy. `getrandom` returns the octet count actually written; a blocking read of 8 bytes returns
# 8, which the fixture confirms before exiting 0. The harness observes exit code 0, proving the
# `syscall3` intrinsic plus the `ptr_word` bridge (§1.2) issued the real instruction against a
# WRITABLE buffer and the kernel honoured it.

Feature: sys_getrandom — the §16 ptr_word bridge + syscall3 intrinsic issue x86_64 Linux getrandom(buf, 8, 0)

  Scenario: raw_getrandom_fills_buffer_and_exits_zero — the process fills 8 bytes of entropy and exits 0
    When the program is built and run
    Then it exits 0
